<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rada of the Belarusian Democratic Republic</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1918">1918</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radaa">Rada</a> of the Belarusian Democratic Republic is the council that proclaimed Belarusian independence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a> on 25 March 1918, under German occupation. It issued a series of constituent charters but could not build a lasting state, and after the return of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> it went into exile. It has continued abroad ever since, making it the oldest government in exile in the world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rada-of-the-belarusian-democratic-republic.jpg" alt="Rada of the Belarusian Democratic Republic" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: BetacommandBot · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Belarus_(1918,_1991%E2%80%931995).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radaa">Rada</a> grew out of the council of the First All-Belarusian Congress, whose delegates met in secret after the Bolshevik authorities of the Western Oblast had dispersed the Congress by force. When the Germans invaded in February 1918 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> withdrew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a>, its leaders stayed and set up provisional institutions. In March the council was expanded into the temporary legislature of the new republic, and on 25 March it had 77 members: 36 elected at the Congress, representatives of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>'s Belarusians, of the Russian, Polish and Jewish minorities, of local authorities and of the major cities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>On the day of independence the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radaa">Rada</a> sent an appeal to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-emperor">German Emperor</a> asking for protection in union with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>. The appeal split the council: Paluta Badunova and Tamaš Hryb left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-secretariat">People's Secretariat</a> in protest and formed the Belarusian Socialist-Revolutionary Party. Germany never recognised the republic, but the Rada began to organise local bodies, an army and schools, and made diplomatic contacts with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, the Ukrainian People's Republic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, the Baltic States and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. As Bolshevik armies approached, it moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grodno">Hrodna</a> and finally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaunas">Kaunas</a>.</p><p>In exile it rejected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozef-pilsudski">Jozef Pilsudski</a>'s offer of purely cultural autonomy, signed a partnership treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> on 11 November 1920 and never recognised the Belarusian SSR. Several members returned home in 1925; most, among them the former prime minister Vaclau Lastouski, were killed in the Soviet terror of the 1930s. In June 1941 its president Vasil Zacharka wished Hitler victory, but he later turned against the Germans and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radaa">Rada</a> refused to recognise the Belarusian Central Rada. Zacharka and Larysa Hienijuš issued papers that saved the Jewish Wolfsohn family.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After 1945 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radaa">Rada</a> worked from western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and later from the USA, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and the United Kingdom, lobbying Western governments and helping create the Belarusian service of Radio Free Europe in the 1950s. In 1991 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> adopted its symbols, the Pahonia and the white-red-white flag, and in 1993 members joined the official celebrations of the republic's 75th anniversary in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a>. The Rada declined to hand its mandate to a parliament first elected under Soviet rule. Ivonka Survilla became its president in 1997, and in June 2023 the Belarusian KGB declared it an extremist formation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rada of the Belarusian Democratic Republic” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Russian Revolution Belarusian activists formed a <b>Rada</b> in Minsk.</blockquote><a href="/state/belarusian-peoples-republic">Belarusian People's Republic</a></li></ul></section>
